# ==============================================================================
# bat/config.darwin — bat on macOS (a cat clone with syntax highlighting)
# ==============================================================================
#
# WHICH FILE IS THIS
#   The macOS variant. bat's format has no conditionals, and the theme setting
#   differs per platform. The package therefore ships `config.darwin` and
#   `config.linux`, and install.sh symlinks ~/.config/bat/config at the right
#   one. The two files are identical except for the Theme block; add a new
#   option to both. See ../../../README.md § Per-OS configuration.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   bat's configuration, read from ~/.config/bat/config. Each line is one
#   command-line argument exactly as typed, not a key/value pair. Lines
#   starting with `#` are comments. Arguments given on the command line
#   override this file.
#
#   FORMAT TRAP: a value containing spaces must be quoted, because each line
#   is split into arguments the way a shell would. `--theme-dark=Catppuccin
#   Mocha` becomes two arguments and bat treats "Mocha" as a file to display.
#   Write `--theme-dark="Catppuccin Mocha"`.
#
# WHERE ELSE bat IS USED
#   The MANPAGER set in ../../../fish/.config/fish/conf.d/10-environment.fish
#   pipes man pages through bat, so the theme and text settings apply there
#   too. That command passes `--plain --language man`, which overrides
#   `--style` and the syntax mappings. Neovim does not use bat.
#
#   Full option list: `bat --help`. Themes: `bat --list-themes`.
# ==============================================================================

# --- Theme --------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: `auto:system` reads the macOS light/dark preference and picks
#       --theme-light or --theme-dark to match.
# WHY : Every tool here follows the system appearance; see
#       ../../../README.md § Light and dark. `auto:system` is macOS-only. The
#       `ansi` theme would also follow the terminal, and match Ghostty's
#       palette exactly, but it renders with 16 colours, which flattens the
#       highlighting.
# HOW : Force one theme with `dark` or `light`, use `--theme="ansi"` for the
#       terminal palette, or pick any name from `bat --list-themes`.
# default: auto (detect from the terminal's colours)
--theme=auto:system
--theme-dark="Catppuccin Mocha"
--theme-light="Catppuccin Latte"

# --- Decorations ----------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Which parts of bat's frame to draw around the file contents.
# WHY : Line numbers to refer to, Git change marks in the gutter, a filename
#       header, and the grid. The grid's vertical rule separates the numbers
#       from the code, so a line number does not read as part of the line. Its
#       horizontal rules frame the file top and bottom, so consecutive `bat`
#       calls stay separate. It costs one column of width and three rules.
# HOW : Other components: rule, snip, header-filename, header-filesize.
#       `--style=plain` (or `-p` on the command line) turns all of it off.
# default: the `default` preset (bat's recommended components)
--style=numbers,changes,header,grid

# --- Text rendering ---------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Emit ANSI italics where the theme uses them (comments, mostly).
# WHY : Some terminals render italics as inverse video, so bat leaves them off.
#       Ghostty renders them correctly, and JetBrains Mono has an italic face.
# default: never
--italic-text=always

# WHAT: Display width of a tab character.
# WHY : Matches `tab_size` in Zed and `shiftwidth` in Neovim.
# default: 4
--tabs=4

# --- Syntax mappings ---------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT: Force a syntax for files bat cannot identify from their name.
# WHY : The Ghostty files carry a `.ghostty` extension that bat has no syntax
#       for and no built-in mapping to, so they render as plain text; INI is
#       the closest match. One rule covers config.ghostty and both per-OS
#       includes. bat maps the deployed ~/.config/git/config to its Git Config
#       syntax on its own, but not the repo copy or `config.work`, so both are
#       mapped here.
# HOW : `--map-syntax='<glob>:<syntax name>'`. A glob with no `/` is matched
#       against the filename alone; one that contains `/` is matched against
#       the full path, where `*` does not cross `/`, so `**/` matches any
#       parent directory. List syntax names with `bat --list-languages`.
--map-syntax=*.ghostty:INI
--map-syntax="**/git/config:Git Config"
--map-syntax="**/git/config.work:Git Config"

# --- Documented extension points (inactive) --------------------------------------------
# WHAT: When to page output. `auto` pages only when the content is taller than
#       the terminal.
# default: auto
# --paging=auto
#
# WHAT: Line wrapping: auto, never, or character.
# default: auto
# --wrap=auto
#
# WHAT: The pager command. By default bat runs `less` with the flags it needs;
#       arguments given here are passed as written.
# default: less
# --pager="less -FR"
